            },
            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nBefore you start, we recommend reading *Learning the Multiplication Table (4 and 5)* (<htcite0>), where you learned how to multiply by four and by five.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "3bc9800d-aa41-4930-84f4-749eb6e40657",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Learning the Multiplication Table (4 and 5)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nA multiplication table is like a map of answers: once we know the map, we no longer need to count everything again. Today we will learn two more of these maps — the table of six (6) and the table of seven (7). Remember from the previous text that multiplying by four and by five means adding the same number four or five times. The result of a multiplication is called the **product**.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "These two tables also grow with small steps: counting by sixes — 6, 12, 18, 24, 30... — and counting by sevens — 7, 14, 21, 28, 35... —. To learn the table of six we will look at a flower with six petals, and to learn the table of seven we will use the seven days of the week. Keep that counting rhythm in mind. Let's begin!",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Growing by six and by seven</b><br /><br />Pause and think: does the table of six grow by six each time? Does the table of seven grow by seven each time?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_six",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Multiplying by Six (6)\n\nNow we turn to the table of six. Multiplying by six means adding the same number six times. Look at a flower: when it has six petals, its petals form a natural group of six. One flower shows 6 petals. Two flowers show 6 + 6 = 12 petals. We are counting by sixes: 6, 12, 18, 24, 30... Each step is one more multiplication: 6 × 1 = 6, 6 × 2 = 12, 6 × 3 = 18, and so on.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"imgcenterresponsive\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 360 230\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A flower with six petals around its yellow center\"><g stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M180 110 L180 216\"/><path d=\"M180 170 Q148 152 136 168 Q156 188 180 182\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/><path d=\"M180 190 Q212 172 224 188 Q204 206 180 202\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/></g><g fill=\"#f8bbd0\" stroke=\"#ad1457\" stroke-width=\"3\"><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(60 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(120 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(180 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(240 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(300 180 110)\"/></g><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"110\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#ffe082\" stroke=\"#f57f17\" stroke-width=\"3\"/></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 1</b>: A flower with six petals around its center. Each petal is one unit of our group of six.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Counting the Petals\n\nOne flower has 6 petals. Two flowers have 6 + 6 = 12 petals. Three flowers have 6 + 6 + 6 = 18 petals. Each new flower adds 6 more petals, so each step is one more multiplication: 6 × 1 = 6, 6 × 2 = 12, 6 × 3 = 18, and so on.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"imgcenterresponsive\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 240\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Five flowers of different colors, each with six petals\"><g><g transform=\"translate(-120 0)\"><g stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M180 110 L180 216\"/><path d=\"M180 170 Q148 152 136 168 Q156 188 180 182\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/><path d=\"M180 190 Q212 172 224 188 Q204 206 180 202\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/></g><g fill=\"#f8bbd0\" stroke=\"#ad1457\" stroke-width=\"3\"><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(60 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(120 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(180 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(240 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(300 180 110)\"/></g><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"110\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#ffe082\" stroke=\"#f57f17\" stroke-width=\"3\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(10 0)\"><g stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M180 110 L180 216\"/><path d=\"M180 170 Q148 152 136 168 Q156 188 180 182\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/><path d=\"M180 190 Q212 172 224 188 Q204 206 180 202\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/></g><g fill=\"#bbdefb\" stroke=\"#1565c0\" stroke-width=\"3\"><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(60 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(120 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(180 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(240 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(300 180 110)\"/></g><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"110\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#ffe082\" stroke=\"#f57f17\" stroke-width=\"3\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(140 0)\"><g stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M180 110 L180 216\"/><path d=\"M180 170 Q148 152 136 168 Q156 188 180 182\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/><path d=\"M180 190 Q212 172 224 188 Q204 206 180 202\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/></g><g fill=\"#ffe0b2\" stroke=\"#ef6c00\" stroke-width=\"3\"><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(60 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(120 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(180 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(240 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(300 180 110)\"/></g><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"110\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#ffe082\" stroke=\"#f57f17\" stroke-width=\"3\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(270 0)\"><g stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M180 110 L180 216\"/><path d=\"M180 170 Q148 152 136 168 Q156 188 180 182\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/><path d=\"M180 190 Q212 172 224 188 Q204 206 180 202\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/></g><g fill=\"#e1bee7\" stroke=\"#6a1b9a\" stroke-width=\"3\"><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(60 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(120 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(180 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(240 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(300 180 110)\"/></g><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"110\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#ffe082\" stroke=\"#f57f17\" stroke-width=\"3\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(400 0)\"><g stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M180 110 L180 216\"/><path d=\"M180 170 Q148 152 136 168 Q156 188 180 182\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/><path d=\"M180 190 Q212 172 224 188 Q204 206 180 202\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/></g><g fill=\"#c8e6c9\" stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\"><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(60 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(120 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(180 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(240 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(300 180 110)\"/></g><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"110\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#ffe082\" stroke=\"#f57f17\" stroke-width=\"3\"/></g></g></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 2</b>: Five flowers of different colors, each with six petals.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "Five flowers of different colors, each with six petals.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_lab_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_lab question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-microscope\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_lab\"><b>Practice 1: Petals in a group</b><br />Look at Figure 2: it shows 5 flowers, and together they have 6 + 6 + 6 + 6 + 6 = 30 petals. Each flower has 6 petals. Suppose we pick a bunch of flowers and want to know how many petals they have in all. Try different numbers of flowers:<br />• 1 flower: 6 × 1 = 6 petals.<br />• 2 flowers: 6 × 2 = 12 petals.<br />• 4 flowers: 6 × 4 = 24 petals.<br />• 5 flowers: 6 × 5 = 30 petals.<br />Do you see the pattern? Each new flower adds 6 more petals. That is what we call counting by sixes: 6, 12, 18, 24, 30!</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### A Formula for the Table of Six\n\nWe can write this rule as a formula that works for every number **N**:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>6</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 1</b>: Multiplying any number by six is the same as adding it six times.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- Read it aloud: “six times N equals N plus N plus N plus N plus N plus N.”\n- The product is always N added to itself six times.\n- **N** is a placeholder for any number you like, so one rule covers every multiplication by six at once.\n- If **N** is 0, the product is 0; if **N** is 1, the product is 6 — exactly what we saw in the table of zero and the table of one.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Now try it yourself: hover the mouse over each multiplication in the box on the left. As you do, the table on the right highlights the matching row, so you can watch every answer for the table of six appear.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div id=\"mParent6\" style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: Is 6 × 3 equal to 3 × 2 × 3?</b><br /><br />Pause and think: we know that 6 = 3 × 2. Does 6 × 3 give the same result as 3 × 2 × 3?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Here is the answer to our reflection: yes! The number 6 is a multiple of 3 and 2, because 3 × 2 = 6. So 6 × 3 is the same as (3 × 2) × 3, which gives 3 × 2 × 3 = 18. Counting by sixes three times also gives 6, 12, 18, so 6 × 3 = 18. The result is the same (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ae53cf73-8e7d-4a2c-80a3-1cb79b7c48bc",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Inversion",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_seven",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Multiplying by Seven (7)\n\nNow we turn to the table of seven. Multiplying by seven means adding the same number seven times. A week in the Gregorian calendar has seven days, always in the same order: Sunday, Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday and Saturday (<htcite0>). One week has 7 days. Two weeks have 7 + 7 = 14 days. We are counting by sevens: 7, 14, 21, 28, 35... Each step is one more multiplication: 7 × 1 = 7, 7 × 2 = 14, 7 × 3 = 21, and so on.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b504be6f-2380-4319-add3-c0682c07e886",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Our Week",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"imgcenterresponsive\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 132\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"The seven days of a week in the Gregorian calendar\"><g font-family=\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><g fill=\"#e3f2fd\" stroke=\"#1565c0\" stroke-width=\"3\"><rect x=\"8\" y=\"14\" width=\"80\" height=\"104\" rx=\"10\"/><rect x=\"98\" y=\"14\" width=\"80\" height=\"104\" rx=\"10\"/><rect x=\"188\" y=\"14\" width=\"80\" height=\"104\" rx=\"10\"/><rect x=\"278\" y=\"14\" width=\"80\" height=\"104\" rx=\"10\"/><rect x=\"368\" y=\"14\" width=\"80\" height=\"104\" rx=\"10\"/><rect x=\"458\" y=\"14\" width=\"80\" height=\"104\" rx=\"10\"/><rect x=\"548\" y=\"14\" width=\"80\" height=\"104\" rx=\"10\"/></g><g fill=\"#0d47a1\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"bold\"><text x=\"48\" y=\"56\">1</text><text x=\"138\" y=\"56\">2</text><text x=\"228\" y=\"56\">3</text><text x=\"318\" y=\"56\">4</text><text x=\"408\" y=\"56\">5</text><text x=\"498\" y=\"56\">6</text><text x=\"588\" y=\"56\">7</text></g><g fill=\"#1565c0\" font-size=\"18\"><text x=\"48\" y=\"90\">Sun</text><text x=\"138\" y=\"90\">Mon</text><text x=\"228\" y=\"90\">Tue</text><text x=\"318\" y=\"90\">Wed</text><text x=\"408\" y=\"90\">Thu</text><text x=\"498\" y=\"90\">Fri</text><text x=\"588\" y=\"90\">Sat</text></g></g></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 3</b>: The seven days of a week in the Gregorian calendar. Each day is one unit of our group of seven.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Counting the Days of the Week\n\nOne week has 7 days. Two weeks have 7 + 7 = 14 days. Three weeks have 7 + 7 + 7 = 21 days. Each new week adds 7 more days, so each step is one more multiplication: 7 × 1 = 7, 7 × 2 = 14, 7 × 3 = 21, and so on.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"imgcenterresponsive\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 230\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A calendar with four weeks and 28 days\"><g font-family=\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"122.5\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0d47a1\">Sun</text><text x=\"187.5\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0d47a1\">Mon</text><text x=\"252.5\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0d47a1\">Tue</text><text x=\"317.5\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0d47a1\">Wed</text><text x=\"382.5\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0d47a1\">Thu</text><text x=\"447.5\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0d47a1\">Fri</text><text x=\"512.5\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0d47a1\">Sat</text><rect x=\"90\" y=\"40\" width=\"65\" height=\"42\" fill=\"#e3f2fd\" stroke=\"#1565c0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"155\" y=\"40\" width=\"65\" height=\"42\" fill=\"#e3f2fd\" stroke=\"#1565c0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"220\" y=\"40\" width=\"65\" height=\"42\" fill=\"#e3f2fd\" stroke=\"#1565c0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"285\" y=\"40\" width=\"65\" height=\"42\" fill=\"#e3f2fd\" stroke=\"#1565c0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"350\" y=\"40\" width=\"65\" height=\"42\" fill=\"#e3f2fd\" stroke=\"#1565c0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"415\" y=\"40\" width=\"65\" height=\"42\" fill=\"#e3f2fd\" stroke=\"#1565c0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"480\" y=\"40\" width=\"65\" height=\"42\" fill=\"#e3f2fd\" stroke=\"#1565c0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"90\" y=\"82\" width=\"65\" height=\"42\" fill=\"#e8f5e9\" stroke=\"#1565c0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"155\" y=\"82\" width=\"65\" height=\"42\" fill=\"#e8f5e9\" stroke=\"#1565c0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"220\" y=\"82\" width=\"65\" height=\"42\" fill=\"#e8f5e9\" stroke=\"#1565c0\" stro