            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_lab_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_lab question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-microscope\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_lab\"><b>Practice 2: Days in a group of weeks</b><br />Look at Figure 4: it shows 4 weeks, and together they have 7 + 7 + 7 + 7 = 28 days. Each week has 7 days. Suppose we want to know how many days there are in several weeks. Try different numbers of weeks:<br />• 1 week: 7 × 1 = 7 days.<br />• 2 weeks: 7 × 2 = 14 days.<br />• 4 weeks: 7 × 4 = 28 days.<br />• 5 weeks: 7 × 5 = 35 days.<br />Do you see the pattern? Each new week adds 7 more days. That is what we call counting by sevens: 7, 14, 21, 28, 35!</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### A Formula for the Table of Seven\n\nWe can write this rule as a formula that works for every number **N**:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>7</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 2</b>: Multiplying any number by seven is the same as adding it seven times.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- Read it aloud: “seven times N equals N plus N plus N plus N plus N plus N plus N.”\n- The product is always N added to itself seven times.\n- **N** is a placeholder for any number you like, so one rule covers every multiplication by seven at once.\n- If **N** is 0, the product is 0; if **N** is 1, the product is 7 — exactly what we saw in the table of zero and the table of one.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Now try it yourself: hover the mouse over each multiplication in the box on the left. As you do, the table on the right highlights the matching row, so you can watch every answer for the table of seven appear.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div id=\"mParent7\" style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 3: How much is 7 × 6?</b><br /><br />Pause and think: count by sevens six times — 7, 14, 21, 28, 35, 42 — or use the table of seven to find the answer.<br /><br />Then check: is the result the same as 6 × 7?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Here is the answer to our reflection: counting by sevens six times gives 7, 14, 21, 28, 35, 42, so 7 × 6 = 42. And 6 × 7 = 42 too (<htcite0>): counting by sixes seven times gives 6, 12, 18, 24, 30, 36, 42. The answer stays the same when we change the order.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ae53cf73-8e7d-4a2c-80a3-1cb79b7c48bc",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Inversion",
                     "date_time": {
