                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nWe have learned two friendly new tables:\n\n- Multiplying by 6 means adding the number six times, which is the same as counting by sixes: 6, 12, 18, 24, 30...\n- Multiplying by 7 means adding the number seven times, which is the same as counting by sevens: 7, 14, 21, 28, 35...\n\nWe used a flower with six petals for the table of six, and the seven days of a week for the table of seven. We also saw that 6 × 7 = 42 and 7 × 6 = 42. Changing the order of the factors does not change the product. (<htcite0>)\n\nKeep practicing with real objects — count the petals of flowers by sixes, count the days of the weeks by sevens — and explore the interactive tables again. You will see these two tables work every time.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ae53cf73-8e7d-4a2c-80a3-1cb79b7c48bc",
